ype {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e5daee-ca5a-5c73-916b-44bccb42c2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721652} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c71beaa-adb7-5262-92fc-9fa1e2247cca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a23c4f7c-1491-53ff-ace9-bdf560f7103c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a23c4f7c-1491-53ff-ace9-bdf560f7103c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721650} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfcddd1a-a24b-5874-b06f-674c9c76b1c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf27ee06-f62c-5a61-ba74-07d91bad3093" :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/fc/fc8694766f760121178e27fdd53d5a05024661addbed5e41e22cd28a3e4bd7b9.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $-2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}$.\n\n$-2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨3,3⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨-1,-4⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b} &=(-2)\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩+(-3)\\cdot ⟨-1,-4⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-6,-6⟩+⟨3,12⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-6+3,-6+12⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0595e664-ac81-5e96-b605-2d594815c806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722314} {:answer/id #uuid "06f1565b-8bde-5892-9473-3819257d0294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722315} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3450ab-2dec-5f1a-8af7-55e45f05a94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722321} {:answer/id #uuid "7be3da81-ecba-5923-964a-77065142f8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722322} {:answer/id #uuid "92bf49bf-88d8-546b-be0e-df96df4bde03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7be3da81-ecba-5923-964a-77065142f8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722322} :answer-field/id #uuid "adad6804-29b0-528b-8070-bf1360207d3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d091586-f4a2-5506-995e-f806367ee1d4" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^3+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The parametrization traverses $C$ once counterclockwise, so the outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y'-Q x')\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P$ and $Q$ are evaluated at $(x(t),y(t))$.\n\nAlong $C$, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP&=8\\cos^3t+\\sin t, & Q&=2\\cos t+\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t, & y'(t)&=\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y'-Q x'&=(8\\cos^3t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\\\\n&\\quad-(2\\cos t+\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\\\\n&=8\\cos^4t+5\\sin t\\cos t+2\\sin^2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\\n&=\\frac14\\left(1+2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3+4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $2\\sin^2t=1-\\cos(2t)$ and $5\\sin t\\cos t=\\frac52\\sin(2t)$. Substituting and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(4+3\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)+\\frac52\\sin(2t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[4t+\\frac32\\sin(2t)+\\frac14\\sin(4t)-\\frac54\\cos(2t)\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=\\left(8\\pi-\\frac54\\right)-\\left(-\\frac54\\right)=8\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f251827-7f94-5f07-8cfa-93b96fc3988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722318} {:answer/id #uuid "50dd8afd-37f3-5019-97b0-ae5f1b5e336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722319} {:answer/id #uuid "52f3e482-0722-520d-a258-e49176e7cec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722320} {:answer/id #uuid "821b5cbb-2ca3-5b7b-8d2e-e62c203b091d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722323} {:answer/id #uuid "98627bba-1e52-56a0-a813-a127f56791b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f3e482-0722-520d-a258-e49176e7cec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722320} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e315c6-4cdf-5fba-a603-38f92e0d6641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e241fe3-ce58-5f7a-b43c-e9c73657b20f" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+2y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The parametrization traverses $C$ once counterclockwise, so the outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y'-Q x')\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P$ and $Q$ are evaluated at $(x(t),y(t))$.\n\nAlong $C$, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP&=2\\cos t+\\sin t, & Q&=2\\cos t+2\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t, & y'(t)&=\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand and collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y'-Q x'&=(2\\cos t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\\\\n&\\quad-(2\\cos t+2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\\\\n&=2\\cos^2t+5\\sin t\\cos t+4\\sin^2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe squared terms have different coefficients, so their sum is not constant. Use $\\cos^2t=(1+\\cos(2t))/2$, $\\sin^2t=(1-\\cos(2t))/2$, and $\\sin t\\cos t=\\sin(2t)/2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(3-\\cos(2t)+\\frac52\\sin(2t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[3t-\\frac12\\sin(2t)-\\frac54\\cos(2t)\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=\\left(6\\pi-\\frac54\\right)-\\left(-\\frac54\\right)=6\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d69f790-687d-5902-b11b-ee9caf0cb2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722317} {:answer/id #uuid "864b9668-c35e-5611-9647-f95ae6d99971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722325} {:answer/id #uuid "97ae29ca-1960-5ab1-aaea-1b87fa687301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722327} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc9d709-1ac8-5f0e-aa60-74b89f9d39a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722333} {:answer/id #uuid "f429e0eb-60fc-53c5-af30-663ab63ee056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ae29ca-1960-5ab1-aaea-1b87fa687301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722327} :answer-field/id #uuid "860d1daf-9914-5c87-839f-66bbfb7565b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1ab0b36-216e-58ae-9195-0145d7f929ee" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,\\mathbf i-(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The parametrization traverses $C$ once counterclockwise, so the outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y'-Q x')\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P$ and $Q$ are evaluated at $(x(t),y(t))$.\n\nAlong $C$, substitute $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=2\\sin t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP&=2\\sin t-2\\cos t, & Q&=-2\\cos t-2\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t, & y'(t)&=2\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two mixed terms cancel. Using $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y'-Q x'&=(2\\sin t-2\\cos t)(2\\cos t)\\\\\n&\\quad-(-2\\cos t-2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\\\\n&=-4\\cos^2t-4\\sin^2t=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\int_0^{2\\pi}-4\\,\\mathrm{d}t=[-4t]_0^{2\\pi}=-8\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe negative sign means that the net flow is inward.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239b957c-9e97-511a-b378-c0fba65f14e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722376} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9254ba-d1c5-5f96-9521-19dbca883d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722378} {:answer/id #uuid "9fed4582-61cc-57ab-ae04-1955de75860e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722387} {:answer/id #uuid "a06c4b3b-14c3-5aab-9894-a9a16a3e8952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722388} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d6f99d-a6ff-5b36-80de-299c381f5fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8d6f99d-a6ff-5b36-80de-299c381f5fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722389} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e62f4c-d0ec-5ec3-9808-4e3875492809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1659809b-b46d-5991-8f07-8824cf5dfac3" :question/problem "![Curve and chosen unit normal](images/fd/fdfa1dc87a032577bfd18d266de5fd11dde014b07e1ecdde5bc8626474158e00.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+2x\\,\\mathbf j$ through the curve $C$ parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$, with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ and a parameterization $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$, evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the curve. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt,\n$$\n\nprovided $\\mathbf n$ is the $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent for increasing $t$.\n\nFor increasing $t$, the curve runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$, with tangent direction $(1,2t)$. Its clockwise normal direction is $(2t,-1)$, agreeing with the pictured normal, so we may use the formula directly.\n\n![Tangent and clockwise normal](images/46/4697accf510b0ea92f64022a1a6f75077d38bf2eed0273ca8a80cf8fb174567c.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into both components of the field and differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t,&y&=t^2,\\\\\nP&=y=t^2,&Q&=2x=2t,\\\\\nx'&=1,&y'&=2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_0^1\\left[(t^2)(2t)-(2t)(1)\\right]\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(2t^3-2t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^4}{2}-t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac12-1=-\\frac12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cda1cd-c43e-5ce1-83fd-edb86ec30d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722377} {:answer/id #uuid "58277d96-1b1c-5351-8583-f80c922389ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722380} {:answer/id #uuid "8581d287-1a83-5e95-a529-397c2649c1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722384} {:answer/id #uuid "89fd1479-74bd-5489-b319-ecc48d392580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722385} {:answer/id #uuid "93522e41-c0aa-5e32-9dca-b770e1533998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8581d287-1a83-5e95-a529-397c2649c1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722384} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e850d7-6448-5ed8-84a1-0a2814f7bc48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba9e8891-55ca-52e6-a725-3169e9060960" :question/problem "![Curve and chosen unit normal](images/78/78d4d59fb9396f20ffad120ba369e913382e1abec1043ef1dfd74f8843b2d90f.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=y^2$ from $(0,0)$ to $(4,2)$, with $0\\le y\\le2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ and a parameterization $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$, evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the curve. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt,\n$$\n\nprovided $\\mathbf n$ is the $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent for increasing $t$.\n\nChoose $y=t$, so $x=t^2$ and $0\\le t\\le2$. This parameterization starts at $(0,0)$ and ends at $(4,2)$. Its tangent direction is $(2t,1)$; rotating clockwise gives $(1,-2t)$, agreeing with the pictured normal.\n\n![Tangent and clockwise normal](images/cb/cb18160c39c739e438aa05ffa1940e5feea9a1edfe9b9d46e177009735c9b8fb.png)\n\nAlong this parameterization,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t^2,&y&=t,\\\\\nP&=y=t,&Q&=x=t^2,\\\\\nx'&=2t,&y'&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_0^2\\left[(t)(1)-(t^2)(2t)\\right]\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2(t-2t^3)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^2}{2}-\\frac{t^4}{2}\\right]_0^2\\\\\n&=2-8=-6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d3c00a-eaaf-5ac9-9a22-6b7823553746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722374} {:answer/id #uuid "1c346f3f-dc18-5b14-8897-3584b44a147f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722375} {:answer/id #uuid "499c9338-573f-5c30-828e-6711719db400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722379} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7572cc-0c57-5bc5-808e-9a94361744a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722381} {:answer/id #uuid "72810ec0-5969-5afa-ba7e-e0cc0d9fd5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d3c00a-eaaf-5ac9-9a22-6b7823553746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722374} :answer-field/id #uuid "14bed8b1-7057-51c8-9e67-e78372b66e7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d886a6c6-a0bb-5272-b664-535f1c2a1ff4" :question/problem "![Curve and chosen unit normal](images/38/3887a4f937c460a8a12ac1570f7f2763986792c2d7b3c627cf441a7fdcbd75ff.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j$ through the line segment $C$ parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+2t\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$, with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown.\n\n*Hint:* When the normal is clockwise from the tangent for increasing $t$, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ and a parameterization $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$, evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the curve. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt,\n$$\n\nprovided $\\mathbf n$ is the $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent for increasing $t$.\n\nFor increasing $t$, the curve runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2)$. Its tangent points in the direction $(1,2)$; a clockwise rotation gives $(2,-1)$, which points toward the pictured normal. Thus the formula has the required sign.\n\n![Tangent and clockwise normal](images/20/2077b2f3007086a0d96cbbded142e8d07856dc7a5f331dbffb54b977bdac5c72.png)\n\nAlong $C$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t,&y&=2t,\\\\\nP&=2,&Q&=1,\\\\\nx'&=1,&y'&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_0^1\\left[2(2)-1(1)\\right]\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 3\\,\\mathrm dt=[3t]_0^1=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sign is positive because the field has a positive component in the chosen normal direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6fbee4-ac3e-5e76-8028-c045ff6e6951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722671} {:answer/id #uuid "45d72834-5993-5afe-b802-ca8646abe1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722673} {:answer/id #uuid "49c0564e-9dbe-5ecf-97a4-1b4eff406746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722674} {:answer/id #uuid "bab31373-f1c4-538d-b434-6d2d0bc8fa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 + 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722684} {:answer/id #uuid "e124677c-f99c-5d9b-81a3-dc8826581b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49c0564e-9dbe-5ecf-97a4-1b4eff406746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722674} :answer-field/id #uuid "f516f37d-62dd-5659-8866-7cdd126d1edb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "123157f0-3b59-5f74-868f-2fa97f1e8e06" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/aa/aa7d3041bfe9f1ccf260ecc694ffcd5084e8b00bd71482ecde611e383d4682cf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 4 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=\\frac{1}{t},\\ y=t$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}},1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(1,\\frac{1}{t^{2}})$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/67/670abe5e761fb3e80045bf6cf153979daa6ce099ecca125621749d794dcc534d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- 4 \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=- \\frac{4}{t} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\left(t\\right)^{2}=2 t^{2} \\\\\nx'&=- \\frac{1}{t^{2}} \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\nAt positive endpoints use $\\ln(1/u)=-\\ln u$, $\\ln u-\\ln v=\\ln(u/v)$, and $\\ln e=1$ as needed; retain the logarithm terms in the final value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}\\left[\\left(- \\frac{4}{t}\\right)(1)-(2 t^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(2 - \\frac{4}{t})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[2 t - 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\\n&=(4 - 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})-(1 + 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}) \\\\\n&=3 - 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57bbe087-7b12-5484-b19b-fe8ee4b5b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722677} {:answer/id #uuid "d62e5cbb-4868-5038-b8b6-245d349702d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722688} {:answer/id #uuid "e108f8a3-f1b1-5719-ad5a-4e9a4d53630c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 e - \\frac{4}{e} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722689} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8b5258-8834-5a34-8f5d-de3d5b36164e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722694} {:answer/id #uuid "fcee4766-ab1e-5d0c-8f12-30176b7b8850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcee4766-ab1e-5d0c-8f12-30176b7b8850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d45dc882-586a-5202-b47b-8db300de9c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40aad8db-170b-5694-ba59-3625a80df439" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/5b/5b30c80b806551a0945d42168bca59d027f13138d6d2b8ea7ae6338a4c4e8d28.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf i+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $1\\le t\\le e$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4 e^{- x})\\,\\mathbf i+(-1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\ y=2 t$, $1\\le t\\le e$. It runs from $(0,2)$ to $(1,2 e)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(\\frac{1}{t},2)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(2,- \\frac{1}{t})$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/e0/e09337a70ae1b90db8ae5b05c10b77ce4e6c57ccd071a9859ada26da55909891.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=\\frac{4}{t} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-1 \\\\\nx'&=\\frac{1}{t} \\\\\ny'&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\nAt positive endpoints use $\\ln(1/u)=-\\ln u$, $\\ln u-\\ln v=\\ln(u/v)$, and $\\ln e=1$ as needed; retain the logarithm terms in the final value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{1}^{e}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{e}\\left[\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)(2)-(-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{e}\\left(\\frac{9}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{1}^{e} \\\\\n&=(9)-(0) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1045d32b-9771-5441-8049-a7afccd34c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722662} {:answer/id #uuid "52a6867e-443f-5749-83a2-44229183a019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722675} {:answer/id #uuid "55f217a6-62a8-5a91-88dc-6d355ffa8cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722676} {:answer/id #uuid "5d262aa0-0130-5f9d-a657-67c3a47a1b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722678} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca22420-88c4-56b3-9345-ee3317469f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a6867e-443f-5749-83a2-44229183a019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722675} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0a4471a-aae2-598d-9db6-5eabe1b971db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e0d4130-7d21-5595-a4e7-99d6e8e4265c" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/49/49f0859775561748963aaa618c05b0f7280c2180f8928a32067dd63b2b1fe8a5.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(2 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 3 y)\\,\\mathbf i+(3 x)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=2 t^{2},\\ y=t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(2,1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(4 t,1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(1,- 4 t)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/a9/a9ff8cd3eab429c5b4d983a20cc46d00213e77defa9b33f3021fc628b892b1e8.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- 3 \\left(t\\right)=- 3 t \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=3 \\left(2 t^{2}\\right)=6 t^{2} \\\\\nx'&=4 t \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(- 3 t)(1)-(6 t^{2})(4 t)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(- 24 t^{3} - 3 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[- 6 t^{4} - \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{15}{2}\\right)-(0) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f585d3c-7c1a-59d6-b279-a61aee22d1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722665} {:answer/id #uuid "293be168-e2d7-504b-b8d3-c4cef2f0738c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722667} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6802bb-6362-5e30-a017-2018b8e2e54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722670} {:answer/id #uuid "8732b2d5-6100-5481-b949-b53bcdf06a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722679} {:answer/id #uuid "edd57ae8-162b-5944-a28a-62da0fa4a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293be168-e2d7-504b-b8d3-c4cef2f0738c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722667} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c60c248-f95c-5bcb-9205-7b21a6f88ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4eb5cb1-40d5-5709-93a8-774d35cade63" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/71/716409dd76b765e46f3c58757ae39d8a6d3b4f1979a19d1025a1b389248df9e2.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x y)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 4 x - 1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=t,\\ y=t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(1,1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(-1,1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/7a/7a994eb1ba591d8c8ea1ff4fb02eb8c183c9f566e9e2a7246854c4f94ae4e6ff.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- \\left(t\\right) \\left(t\\right)=- t^{2} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=- 4 \\left(t\\right) - 1=- 4 t - 1 \\\\\nx'&=1 \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(- t^{2})(1)-(- 4 t - 1)(1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(t^{2} - 4 t - 1)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^{3}}{3} - 2 t^{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right)-(0) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100ef981-aaa9-5a4f-afb2-3ac5661558ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722661} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea1bba4-d936-5f5f-bfa2-e78086a468b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722668} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5fc286-c9a6-50bc-b64f-d27fa8f58ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722669} {:answer/id #uuid "b39f1d71-0824-52dc-b800-41fe8c12a37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722683} {:answer/id #uuid "c433de28-7808-5975-9549-e7a324db6144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "100ef981-aaa9-5a4f-afb2-3ac5661558ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722661} :answer-field/id #uuid "232e239e-26ed-5062-a5d4-138d7bb4b86c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f261d926-f97d-5138-85a4-bc88db4758e0" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/ba/ba1497413ac0f51a0b3f74bacb325a5c73ddf3166cde4be09d1d5b7bcd010b7b.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(t)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4)\\,\\mathbf i+(2)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=t,\\ y=\\frac{1}{t}$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(1,- \\frac{1}{t^{2}})$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(\\frac{1}{t^{2}},1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/d7/d7ea4aed5cad1335458a52c27f75f98e93ab7c3e66ec85a809399e23465aead0.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\\\\nx'&=1 \\\\\ny'&=- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}\\left[(4)\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)-(2)(1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(2 + \\frac{4}{t^{2}})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[2 t - \\frac{4}{t}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\\n&=(2)-(-7) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e3c98e-a41e-5508-a4ec-aa3cda2890d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722720} {:answer/id #uuid "310ad598-4df5-507c-b6da-737867050bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722722} {:answer/id #uuid "8977bc33-fbc3-5244-8226-1ac8b5f14c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722737} {:answer/id #uuid "c99b83b7-6139-5f44-a6dc-92e28afc2264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722740} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0b57de-5683-5b2a-b864-ed6dbf081527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8977bc33-fbc3-5244-8226-1ac8b5f14c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722737} :answer-field/id #uuid "555832ec-0d34-544c-8b1f-8dfff4585b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ce688e4-c7b6-558c-9d06-cfad9e84d72e" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/94/94dfc91c8d9e12a0edacfeebd60106fa15aa86b94e4cbf7fd058b827e23d1cd1.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 t)\\,\\mathbf i+(- t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4)\\,\\mathbf i+(-3)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- 2 t,\\ y=- t$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-4,-2)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-2,-1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(1,-2)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/ea/eaf9df468563558c596a3ef20c8150cab92f40a3f0db52cdb2ccf7ef0a85720d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-3 \\\\\nx'&=-2 \\\\\ny'&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[(4)(-1)-(-3)(-2)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}(10)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[10 t\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=(20)-(0) \\\\\n&=20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fc6a56-44e8-5a41-adae-6c1844f96e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722718} {:answer/id #uuid "10781a3f-7fed-512b-9708-80482e673743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722719} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d512d-45bb-545a-8a93-e91ce9834937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722721} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0e7367-67b0-500e-8207-530b3b4841de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722735} {:answer/id #uuid "c07f1a06-dc90-5578-8a5f-34ba57d0acb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07f1a06-dc90-5578-8a5f-34ba57d0acb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722739} :answer-field/id #uuid "71865666-0639-5b8e-a46b-80f85ebe0c38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66507b1e-148c-50b2-96ec-f1337d41e41f" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/ee/ee6fc7fe45aaf961e48cdee198b4e770c325f4231012ba6f71b060531273b44e.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- t)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(-1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- t,\\ y=2 t$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-2,4)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-1,2)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(-2,-1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/07/073fd5d0a886bf61e2fc8b9c4558aab79c8c2fe1d23aeae9d4102f538667501d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=-2 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-1 \\\\\nx'&=-1 \\\\\ny'&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[(-2)(2)-(-1)(-1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}(5)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[5 t\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=(10)-(0) \\\\\n&=10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a5143fd-2d03-5c58-bc23-d25141b94ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722723} {:answer/id #uuid "68735789-14d1-5531-85d4-eda0937f9e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722730} {:answer/id #uuid "7552b106-4f6a-5018-920c-56c4ce739f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722734} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e34100-dc86-50bb-8ede-f248d327934f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722742} {:answer/id #uuid "f73668c4-5669-5166-b03e-d7df692e3861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7552b106-4f6a-5018-920c-56c4ce739f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722734} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e73ab31-e15c-5ef8-b247-fd1b901c2e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80e13309-1d97-531b-9deb-490f080ce90c" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/6a/6a90dacb53f070474d20a2f241bed17c9366d3bf2fe8875fbd6a4885ba5c7b17.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 t)\\,\\mathbf i+(- t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(-3)\\,\\mathbf i+(3)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- 2 t,\\ y=- t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-2,-1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-2,-1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(-1,2)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/f4/f4360dfd2574be37d49bfed630242fc0310008fc630461b8a3d79a87192722a7.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=-3 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=3 \\\\\nx'&=-2 \\\\\ny'&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(-3)(-1)-(3)(-2)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(9)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[9 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=(9)-(0) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aed6c56-0401-5b0c-b1dd-001932167c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722724} {:answer/id #uuid "5149b1eb-f35c-5081-8829-580ae8bb1994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722725} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3a7448-f51c-58ee-98f7-e861c9d6f4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722731} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8865f9-d321-5c42-a831-314eaed6a9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722738} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a16525-2b61-5616-931f-a57b6bfc3229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5149b1eb-f35c-5081-8829-580ae8bb1994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722725} :answer-field/id #uuid "55191fba-ff70-51c2-9e19-67ff745c564c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8f9d296-ec2a-506e-be95-cfd11c67bf8c" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/7a/7a2389176221c3be048a9106e4f69d2154bf7ba36f6a6d986e062bc86ab61eb4.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 t)\\,\\mathbf i+(- t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2)\\,\\mathbf i+(-4)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- 2 t,\\ y=- t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-2,-1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-2,-1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(1,-2)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/f0/f055ac9fb931827826dc450eefc37d947915ecbe54a18ef0f7037a50eee7d6b9.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-4 \\\\\nx'&=-2 \\\\\ny'&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(2)(-1)-(-4)(-2)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(10)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[10 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=(10)-(0) \\\\\n&=10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2ec690-7e28-5361-9b8c-d994e4cba461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722878} {:answer/id #uuid "11d36e1c-7697-579d-96bb-2cb062599e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722879} {:answer/id #uuid "38693d4f-dabb-58f7-b110-f476a483c396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722884} {:answer/id #uuid "3febcbf1-09c4-5f99-9353-d23cbf87cffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722885} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb79c94-54ca-570d-aeb2-e9352c151882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38693d4f-dabb-58f7-b110-f476a483c396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722884} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4bf66b-53a0-5d45-baea-5ce590b8753f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e187fa6-eab9-547a-be95-72e6b6a7267e" :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 x + y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$, simplify the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=8 t+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[8 t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=16 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=16 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (16 \\pi)=16 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
